   {"headline":"Vol drained on the close — VIX 15.2, down 1.14 pts — while the semi-cap and cybersecurity complexes bled out under a calm tape","tape":[{"label":"VIX","value":"15.2","change":"-1.14 pts","positive":true},{"label":"10Y","value":"4.79","change":"+0 bp","positive":true},{"label":"HY OAS","value":"2.66","change":"+1 bp","positive":false},{"label":"SPY","value":"769.55","change":"","positive":true}],"schedule":[],"cause":{"located":false,"searched":["VIX"]},"sections":[{"title":"Falling vol was the one channel that moved — VIX 15.2, down 1.14 points into the close","body":"The regime is a vol drain against an otherwise inert macro tape. VIXCLS printed 15.2 versus 16.34 the day before, a -1.14 pt move (obs 2026-09-02), and it is the only ranked channel with a real delta: the 10-year sat at 4.79 versus 4.79, a +0 bp non-move (obs 2026-09-02), and HY OAS ticked to 2.66 from 2.65, +1 bp, per ow_macro_rates BAMLH0A0HYM2 (obs 2026-09-02; fredDirect skipped, fetch failed, so this is the daily-mirror series, 5 days behind). Falling vol with credit essentially still and long rates unchanged is a benign-drift tape, not a risk-on impulse — nothing in the rates or credit channels is corroborating a chase. The persistence read holds: no single macro move stood out, and VIXCLS has led for zero sessions, so this is a low-conviction, mean-reverting vol print rather than the start of a trend. The market tide covers today's RTH session dated 2026-09-03, now closed; net call premium ran positive most of the day against negative net put premium, consistent with the vol bleed, but that tape is the previous (completed) session and not a live read."},{"title":"The anomaly is a cross-sector semi/security rout that the index vol never priced","body":"Beside the calm vol channel, the loudest divergence is a violent single-week rotation inside tech that the headline tape hid. Semi-Cap/EDA fell -8.2%, -8.4% vs SPY with 8 of 8 members down, and Cybersecurity fell -9.9%, -10.1% vs SPY with 4 of 4 down (both weekly, asOf 2026-09-03) — the two worst cohorts in the book. Semi-Logic/ASIC (-4.6%, -4.8% vs SPY) and Software/SaaS (-2.7%, -3.0% vs SPY) sit right behind. Yet at the top of the same complex, Devices/Endpoint rose +5.2%, +4.9% vs SPY (TSLA, AAPL), and the Foundation-Model-Proxy megacaps added +1.9%, +1.7% vs SPY — so capital is not leaving tech, it is concentrating in the largest names and gutting the picks-and-shovels and security tiers. Rotation confirms it: XLK is the second-weakest sector at -1.4% (1w, -1.7% vs SPY), while the el-nino-ag-2026 theme basket leads everything at +7.3% excess. That a VIX of 15.2 sits alongside 8-to-10% single-week drawdowns in named cohorts is the tension to watch — index vol is not pricing what the sector tape is doing. The next dated catalysts that bear on it are ADBE and ORCL earnings on 2026-09-10 (implied moves 6.9333% and 9.8619%) and FOMC 9/16, futures-implied via Frenzy Capital at HIKE 60% (snapshot 2026-09-02), not CME FedWatch."}]}
